    "summary": "Soaring Dark Star, pedal‑steel Looks Like Rain & high‑energy second set",
    "blurb": "Features a soaring Dark Star and the rare pedal‑steel rendition of Looks Like Rain, with The Stranger (Two Souls in Communion) highlighted. The second set stays tight through a raucous Not Fade Away > Goin' Down The Road Feeling Bad sandwich. Overall playing is inspired and high‑energy.",
    "review": "The March 23, 1972 Academy of Music show captures the Dead on the cusp of their legendary European tour. Jerry’s guitar soars from the opening China Cat Sunflower into a crystalline Dark Star that stretches into uncharted territory, while his pedal‑steel work on Looks Like Rain adds an unexpected, shimmering texture. Phil is truly on fire, delivering one of his hottest bass performances—especially in the Dark Star and the driving groove behind Looks Like Rain’s backing vocals. Two Souls in Communion (The Stranger) stands out as a soulful centerpiece, and Jack Straw shines as a tight, punchy highlight from the marathon first set.\n\nThe second set is a masterclass in momentum: Truckin' segues into an exuberant Ramble On Rose before the band launches into Dark Star, then rolls straight into a blistering Not Fade Away > Goin' Down The Road Feeling Bad sandwich that ends with Pigpen and Mickey delivering an intense, screaming finale. Throughout, Keith’s organ anchors the early‑’70s sound, while both drummers—Bill Kreutzmann and Mickey Hart—keep the rhythmic engine roaring. In short, this performance is a high‑energy, tightly knit snapshot of the Dead at a creative peak, captured in pristine soundboard fidelity.",
